  "title": "Zig vs Rust vs Go: compile errors compared",
  "content": "zig rust problems have a habit of surfacing mid-migration, right when there's no time to solve them properly. The failure is quiet — no errors, just wrong answers. Here's what changes once the guessing is replaced with measurement.\n\nCompile errors in systems languages are not just annoyances; they encode the language's philosophy about memory, concurrency, and correctness. If you're choosing between Zig, Rust, and Go for a backend service, a CLI tool, or a network daemon, the error messages you'll stare at daily are a practical signal of how much the language will fight you—and where.\n\nThis post compares the three languages through the lens of their most common compile-time and runtime failure modes. It explains what each error actually means, how to fix it, and—crucially—when that fix is a sign you picked the wrong tool for the job. The goal is not to crown a winner but to help you map your project's constraints to the language whose error profile you can live with.\n\n## The error and why it's confusing\n\nStart with a concrete example: a simple function that reads a file into a buffer and returns it. In Go, you might write:\n\n```go\nfunc readFile(path string) ([]byte, error) {\n    data, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n    if err != nil {\n        return nil, err\n    }\n    return data, nil\n}\n```\n\nThis compiles. The error handling is explicit but verbose. Now try the same in Rust:\n\n```rust\nuse std::fs;\n\nfn read_file(path: &str) -> Result<Vec<u8>, std::io::Error> {\n    fs::read(path)\n}\n```\n\nAlso compiles. The `?` operator could shorten it further. Now Zig:\n\n```zig\nconst std = @import(\"std\");\n\nfn readFile(path: []const u8) ![]u8 {\n    const file = try std.fs.cwd().openFile(path, .{});\n    defer file.close();\n    return try file.readToEndAlloc(std.heap.page_allocator, 1 << 20);\n}\n```\n\nThis compiles too—but only if you understand `defer`, allocator choice, and the `!` error union. The confusion begins when you hit errors that are not about syntax but about the language's core model.\n\nFor Go, the most common confusing error is `declared and not used`. It appears when you declare a variable and never read it. Go treats unused local variables as a compile error, not a warning. That's deliberate: it prevents dead code and forces you to clean up. But it also means that during refactoring, you'll often see:\n\n```\n./main.go:10:6: x declared and not used\n```\n\nThe fix is trivial—use `_ = x` or remove the declaration—but the error feels punitive if you're used to languages that warn instead.\n\nFor Rust, the confusion often starts with the borrow checker:\n\n```\nerror[E0502]: cannot borrow `v` as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable\n```\n\nThis is not a syntax error; it's a semantic error about aliasing and mutation. The compiler is telling you that your code violates Rust's ownership rules. The fix requires restructuring data flow, not just adding a semicolon.\n\nFor Zig, the confusion is often about compile-time evaluation and the `comptime` keyword, or about error unions. A typical error:\n\n```\nerror: expected type 'u32', found 'comptime_int'\n```\n\nThis happens when you pass an integer literal to a function expecting a specific integer type. Zig's comptime integer literals are arbitrary precision, but they must coerce to the expected type. The fix is to annotate: `const x: u32 = 42;`.\n\nThe underlying confusion in all three cases is the same: the error is not about what you typed, but about the language's invariants. Go enforces simplicity and explicitness; Rust enforces memory safety without a garbage collector; Zig enforces manual memory management with compile-time checks. Each error is a boundary of that invariant.\n\n## What's actually causing it (the real reason, not the surface symptom)\n\nWhen you see `x declared and not used` in Go, the real cause is not that you forgot to use `x`—it's that Go's compiler is designed to reject code that might indicate a mistake. The Go team has stated that unused variables and imports are compile errors to keep code clean and to avoid the kind of drift that leads to bugs. This is a philosophical choice: Go prioritizes readability and maintainability over flexibility. The error is a feature, not a bug.\n\nIn Rust, `cannot borrow as mutable` is not a limitation but a consequence of the borrow checker's goal: to guarantee memory safety and data-race freedom at compile time. The error means your code has a potential data race or use-after-free. The compiler is preventing you from writing code that would be undefined behavior in C or C++. The fix is to redesign your data ownership—often by using `RefCell`, `Mutex`, or by splitting your data into disjoint borrows.\n\nIn Zig, `expected type 'u32', found 'comptime_int'` reveals that Zig's type system is stricter than C's about integer widths. Zig does not implicitly convert between integer types, even when the value fits. This prevents a class of bugs where a value silently truncates. The error is a nudge to be explicit about your integer sizes, which matters in systems programming where memory layout and overflow behavior are critical.\n\nThe surface symptom in each case is an error message. The real cause is that the language is enforcing a contract that you may not have internalized yet. The fix is not just to silence the error but to understand which contract you're violating and whether you agree with it.\n\n## Fix 1 — the most common cause: fighting the language's core invariant\n\nThe most common cause of these errors is that you're writing code in the style of another language. A Go developer new to Rust might try to share mutable state across goroutines using a `Rc<RefCell<T>>` and hit `Rc` is not `Send`. A Rust developer new to Go might try to use generics heavily and find the syntax clunky. A C developer new to Zig might expect implicit integer conversions and hit `comptime_int` errors.\n\nThe fix is to adopt the language's idioms. For Go, that means embracing explicit error returns and avoiding unnecessary abstractions. For Rust, it means designing your data flow around ownership and borrowing, often using `Arc<Mutex<T>>` for shared mutable state across threads. For Zig, it means being explicit about types and using `@intCast` or `@as` when you need to convert.\n\nHere's a concrete example in Rust: if you have a vector and you want to modify it while iterating, you can't do this:\n\n```rust\nlet mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];\nfor x in &v {\n    v.push(*x); // error: cannot borrow `v` as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable\n}\n```\n\nThe fix is to collect the new elements first:\n\n```rust\nlet mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];\nlet new_elements: Vec<_> = v.iter().map(|x| *x).collect();\nv.extend(new_elements);\n```\n\nOr use indices:\n\n```rust\nlet mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];\nfor i in 0..v.len() {\n    let x = v[i];\n    v.push(x);\n}\n```\n\nThis is not a workaround; it's the correct way to express the algorithm in Rust's ownership model.\n\n## Fix 2 — the less obvious cause: toolchain and build system mismatches\n\nThe second most common cause is a mismatch between the language's toolchain and your environment. For example, in Go, you might see:\n\n```\ngo: cannot find module providing package github.com/foo/bar\n```\n\nThis usually means your `GOPATH` or module cache is misconfigured, or you're offline. The fix is to run `go mod tidy` and ensure your `GOFLAGS` are correct. In Rust, a common error is:\n\n```\nerror: failed to run custom build command for `some-crate v0.1.0`\n```\n\nThis often means a system dependency is missing (e.g., `libssl-dev` on Debian-based systems). The fix is to install the missing library or use a vendored feature. In Zig, you might see:\n\n```\nerror: unable to open file: zig-cache/...\n```\n\nThis can happen if the cache directory is corrupted or if you're using a read-only filesystem. The fix is to clear the cache with `rm -rf zig-cache` and rebuild.\n\nThese errors are less about language design and more about environment. They are often the first hurdle when setting up a project on a new machine or in CI.\n\n## Fix 3 — the environment-specific cause: cross-compilation and target platforms\n\nSystems programming often involves cross-compilation. Go makes this relatively easy with `GOOS` and `GOARCH` environment variables. But you might hit:\n\n```\nbuild constraints exclude all Go files in ...\n```\n\nThis means the files in the directory are not compatible with your target OS/arch. The fix is to add build tags or ensure the files have the correct `//go:build` directives.\n\nRust uses target triples. A common error is:\n\n```\nerror: linking with `cc` failed: exit status: 1\n```\n\nThis often means you're missing a cross-compilation toolchain (e.g., `gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu`). The fix is to install the toolchain and set the linker in `.cargo/config.toml`.\n\nZig is designed for cross-compilation and bundles its own C compiler. But you might still see:\n\n```\nerror: unable to find dynamic linker for target\n```\n\nThis means the target triple is not fully supported or the linker is missing. The fix is to use a supported target or provide the linker path.\n\nCross-compilation errors are particularly painful because they often surface only in CI or when deploying to a different architecture. The fix is to codify your build environment—use Docker, Nix, or a CI matrix that tests all target platforms.\n\n## How to verify the fix worked\n\nAfter applying a fix, verify it by reproducing the original error and confirming it's gone. For Go, run `go build ./...` and ensure no errors. For Rust, run `cargo check` (faster than `cargo build`) and then `cargo test`. For Zig, run `zig build` or `zig build test`.\n\nBut compilation success is not enough. You should also run your test suite, preferably with sanitizers. In Rust, `cargo test` with `RUSTFLAGS=\"-Z sanitizer=address\"` on nightly can catch memory issues. In Go, `go test -race` enables the race detector. In Zig, you can use `std.testing.allocator` to detect leaks in tests.\n\nFor cross-compilation, verify by running the binary on the target platform or in an emulator. If you can't run it, at least check the binary format with `file` and ensure it matches the target architecture.\n\n## How to prevent this from happening again\n\nPrevention starts with understanding your language's invariants and encoding them in your development workflow. For Go, enable `go vet` and `staticcheck` in your editor and CI. For Rust, use `clippy` and `rustfmt`. For Zig, use `zig fmt` and consider `zig ast-check`.\n\nMore importantly, write tests that exercise the boundaries: integer overflow, null pointers, concurrent access, and cross-platform behavior. In Go, table-driven tests with `t.Parallel()` can catch race conditions. In Rust, property-based testing with `proptest` or `quickcheck` can explore edge cases. In Zig, `comptime` tests can verify compile-time invariants.\n\nFinally, document your build environment. A `Dockerfile` or `flake.nix` that pins toolchain versions and system dependencies will save hours of debugging when a new team member joins or when CI breaks.\n\n## Related errors you might hit next\n\nOnce you've resolved the immediate error, you may encounter these related issues:\n\n- **Go:** `panic: runtime error: index out of range` — often caused by off-by-one errors in slice indexing. Fix with bounds checks or use `for i, v := range slice`.\n- **Go:** `fatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!` — indicates a deadlock in channel operations. Use `go vet` or the race detector.\n- **Rust:** `error[E0382]: use of moved value` — you're trying to use a value after it's been moved. Fix by cloning or using references.\n- **Rust:** `error[E0277]: the trait bound `T: Send` is not satisfied` — you're trying to send a non-Send type across threads. Wrap in `Arc<Mutex<T>>` or use `std::sync::mpsc`.\n- **Zig:** `error: integer overflow` — Zig checks integer overflow in safe modes. Use `@addWithOverflow` or `+%` for wrapping arithmetic.\n- **Zig:** `error: unreachable code` — the compiler detected code that can never execute. Remove it or adjust your logic.\n\nThese errors are not random; they are consequences of the same invariants. Learning to recognize them quickly is a sign you're internalizing the language.\n\n## When none of these work: escalation path\n\nIf you've tried the fixes and still can't resolve the error, the next step is to isolate the problem. Create a minimal reproducible example (MRE) in a new project. For Go, use `go mod init` and copy the failing code. For Rust, use `cargo new` and add dependencies one by one. For Zig, use `zig init-exe` and strip down your code.\n\nIf the MRE still fails, search for the exact error message in the language's issue tracker or forum. For Go, the official issue tracker is on GitHub. For Rust, the Rust Users Forum and the `rust-lang/rust` repository. For Zig, the Zig GitHub issues and the Zig Discord/IRC.\n\nWhen asking for help, include the full error message, the toolchain version (`go version`, `rustc --version`, `zig version`), the target platform, and the MRE. This will get you a faster response.\n\nIf the error is due to a bug in the language or a library, consider filing an issue. If it's a limitation, you may need to work around it or choose a different tool. That's not failure; it's engineering.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Why does Go complain about unused variables but not unused functions?**\nGo's compiler treats unused local variables and imports as errors to prevent dead code and accidental shadowing. Unused functions are allowed because they might be part of an exported API or used via reflection. The design choice prioritizes local clarity over global completeness.\n\n**How do I fix `cannot borrow as mutable` in Rust when I need to modify a collection while iterating?**\nYou can't mutate a collection while holding an immutable borrow. Instead, collect the changes into a temporary vector and apply them after the loop, or use indices to access elements. Alternatively, use `RefCell` for interior mutability if you're single-threaded, or `Mutex` for multi-threaded.\n\n**What does `comptime_int` mean in Zig and how do I resolve the error?**\n`comptime_int` is an arbitrary-precision integer known at compile time. When you pass it to a function expecting a fixed-width integer (e.g., `u32`), Zig requires an explicit cast. Use `@as(u32, 42)` or annotate the variable type: `const x: u32 = 42;`.\n\n**When should I choose Zig over Rust or Go for a new project?**\nChoose Zig when you need precise control over memory layout, want to avoid hidden allocations, and are comfortable with manual memory management. Zig excels at cross-compilation and C interoperability. Rust is better when you want memory safety without a garbage collector and a rich ecosystem. Go is best for rapid development of networked services where simplicity and fast compile times matter more than zero-cost abstractions.\n\n## Closing action\n\nOpen your project's `go.mod`, `Cargo.toml`, or `build.zig` and check the toolchain version. Run `go version`, `rustc --version`, or `zig version` and compare it to the version your CI uses. A mismatch there is the most common cause of \"works on my machine\" build failures—and it takes 30 seconds to rule out.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
